\title[The Non-Backtracking Trace Formula]{The Non-Backtracking Trace Formula:\\
Counting Rooted Closed Non-Backtracking Walks\\ by Powers of the Hashimoto Matrix}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph and let $B$ denote its Hashimoto
(non-backtracking) matrix, the zero--one matrix indexed by the $2|E|$ darts of $G$ whose
$(d,e)$ entry is $1$ precisely when the dart $e$ continues the dart $d$ without an
immediate reversal. We establish the identity
$\tr(B^{n})=\#\{\text{rooted closed non-backtracking walks of length }n\}$ for every
$n\ge 0$, in three equivalent encodings: lists of $n+1$ darts with coinciding endpoints,
cyclic words of $n$ darts, and cyclic vertex sequences $(u_{1},\dots,u_{n})$ with
$u_{i}\sim u_{i+1}$ and $u_{i+2}\neq u_{i}$ read modulo $n$. Because the non-backtracking
succession relation is not symmetric, the classical adjacency-matrix walk-counting theorem
does not apply, and walk counting for an arbitrary decidable relation on a finite index set
is developed as a prerequisite. From the counting theorem we deduce a dictionary between
the sequence $(\tr(B^{n}))_{n\ge 1}$ and the cycle structure of $G$: the universal
vanishing $\tr(B)=\tr(B^{2})=0$; the evaluation
$\tr(B^{3})=6\cdot\#\{\text{triangles}\}$; the row-sum identity
$\sum_{e}B_{d,e}=\deg(\deghead d)-1$ and the growth bound $\tr(B^{n})\le 2|E|q^{n}$ for
$(q+1)$-regular graphs; the evenness of every trace, with its algebraic source
$JBJ=B^{\T}$; the criterion that $G$ is a forest if and only if $\tr(B^{n})=0$ for all
$n\ge 1$; the girth criterion $g(G)=\min\{n\ge 1:\tr(B^{n})\neq 0\}$ refined by
$2g(G)\le\tr(B^{g(G)})$; and monotonicity of the whole sequence under subgraph inclusion.
Closed-form evaluations for $K_{3}$, $K_{4}$, $C_{5}$, paths and the Petersen graph are
given.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Non-backtracking walk, Hashimoto matrix, trace formula, girth, acyclicity,
